 {
  "id": "mclaughlin",
  "cat": "legislativo",
  "name": "Matt McLaughlin",
  "fn": "Matt",
  "ln": "McLaughlin",
  "title": "Professional Staff Member",
  "orgId": "",
  "org": "House Committee on Foreign Affairs",
  "dep": "",
  "es": "Matt McLaughlin se desempeña como miembro del personal profesional del Comité de Asuntos Exteriores de la Cámara de Representantes de los Estados Unidos, el comité encargado de supervisar la política exterior estadounidense, cargo que asumió en enero de 2025. En sus antecedentes formativos, cursó la educación secundaria en la escuela Sidwell Friends, en Washington (2012), y obtuvo una licenciatura en economía, ciencias políticas y español en Bates College, en Maine (2016). En su trayectoria profesional, su primer contacto con el Congreso fue como pasante en la oficina de la senadora Jeanne Shaheen, demócrata por Nuevo Hampshire (2013), cuando aún era estudiante universitario. Ya graduado, fue pasante del Comité de Normas de la Cámara de Representantes (2019) y del Comité de Asuntos Exteriores (2020), al que luego se incorporó como asociado de personal (2020). Posteriormente, pasó a la oficina del representante Gerry Connolly, demócrata por Virginia, como asistente legislativo (2021) y ascendió a asesor sénior de política exterior (2023), antes de regresar al Comité de Asuntos Exteriores en su cargo actual. En el plano personal, su formación en español y su recorrido de más de una década en el Congreso, desde pasante hasta miembro del personal profesional, reflejan una vocación sostenida por la política exterior.",
  "en": "Matt McLaughlin serves as a Professional Staff Member of the House Foreign Affairs Committee (HFAC) of the United States House of Representatives, the committee that oversees United States foreign policy, a position he assumed in January 2025. In his educational background, he completed his secondary education at Sidwell Friends School in Washington (2012) and earned a bachelor's degree in economics, political science, and Spanish from Bates College in Maine (2016). His first contact with Congress came as an intern in the office of Senator Jeanne Shaheen, Democrat of New Hampshire (2013), while still a university student. After graduating, he interned with the House Committee on Rules (2019) and with the House Foreign Affairs Committee (HFAC) (2020), which he then joined as a Staff Associate (2020). He subsequently moved to the office of Representative Gerry Connolly, Democrat of Virginia, as a Legislative Assistant (2021) and rose to Senior Adviser for Foreign Policy (2023), before returning to the House Foreign Affairs Committee in his current role. On a personal note, his training in Spanish and his path of more than a decade in Congress, from intern to Professional Staff Member, reflect a sustained vocation for foreign policy."
 },
 {
  "id": "polga-hecimovich",
  "cat": "academia",
  "name": "John Polga-Hecimovich",
  "fn": "John",
  "ln": "Polga-Hecimovich",
  "title": "Associate Professor of Political Science",
  "orgId": "U.S. Naval Academy",
  "org": "U.S. Naval Academy",
  "dep": "Department of Political Science",
  "es": "John Polga-Hecimovich se desempeña como profesor asociado (Associate Professor) de Ciencia Política en la Academia Naval de los Estados Unidos (United States Naval Academy), donde también es codirector del Forum for Latin American Studies, cargos que ejerce desde 2021. En sus antecedentes formativos y profesionales, en orden cronológico, obtuvo la licenciatura en Gobierno y Español en Dartmouth College (2004) y la maestría en Estudios Latinoamericanos en la Universidad Andina Simón Bolívar, en Quito (2006). Publicó el libro Políticos, militares y ciudadanos: un análisis de las caídas presidenciales en Ecuador (1997-2005) (2010). Fue profesor visitante en la Facultad Latinoamericana de Ciencias Sociales (FLACSO), en Quito (2013), y en Wake Forest University (2015). Obtuvo el doctorado en Ciencia Política en la University of Pittsburgh (2015) y fue profesor asistente visitante en el College of William and Mary (2016). Posteriormente, se incorporó a la Academia Naval de los Estados Unidos como profesor asistente (Assistant Professor) (2016) y fue promovido a profesor asociado (2021). Fue investigador visitante en la Universidad de Salamanca, en el Instituto de Iberoamérica (2023). Es Senior Fellow del Jack D. Gordon Institute for Public Policy de Florida International University. En 2025 coeditó, junto con Raúl Sánchez Urribarri, el libro Authoritarian Consolidation in Times of Crisis: Venezuela under Nicolás Maduro. Su investigación se centra en la política latinoamericana, en especial en Venezuela y Ecuador, las caídas presidenciales y la consolidación autoritaria. En el plano humano, su trayectoria refleja una larga vinculación con América Latina, desde su formación en Quito hasta su trabajo académico sobre la región, y una labor sostenida de formación de futuros oficiales en la Academia Naval.",
  "en": "John Polga-Hecimovich serves as an Associate Professor of Political Science at the United States Naval Academy (USNA) and co-director of the Forum for Latin American Studies (FLAS), a position he has held since 2021. In his academic and professional background, in chronological order, he earned a bachelor's degree in Government and Spanish from Dartmouth College (2004) and a master's degree in Latin American Studies from the Universidad Andina Simón Bolívar, in Quito (2006). He published the book Políticos, militares y ciudadanos: un análisis de las caídas presidenciales en Ecuador (1997-2005) (2010). He was a visiting professor at the Facultad Latinoamericana de Ciencias Sociales (FLACSO), in Quito (2013), and at Wake Forest University (2015). He earned a PhD in Political Science from the University of Pittsburgh (2015) and was a visiting assistant professor at the College of William and Mary (2016). He then joined the United States Naval Academy as an Assistant Professor (2016) and was promoted to Associate Professor (2021). He was a visiting researcher at the University of Salamanca, at the Instituto de Iberoamérica (2023). He is a Senior Fellow of the Jack D. Gordon Institute for Public Policy at Florida International University (FIU). In 2025, he co-edited, with Raúl Sánchez Urribarri, the book Authoritarian Consolidation in Times of Crisis: Venezuela under Nicolás Maduro. His research focuses on Latin American politics, especially Venezuela and Ecuador, presidential removals, and authoritarian consolidation. On the human plane, his career reflects a long engagement with Latin America, from his studies in Quito to his academic work on the region, and a sustained commitment to educating future officers at the Naval Academy.",
  "photo": "data:image/jpeg;base64,"
 },
 {
  "id": "grady",
  "cat": "ejecutivo",
  "name": "Dana W. Grady",
  "fn": "Dana W.",
  "ln": "Grady",
  "title": "Regional Advisor for Latin America",
  "orgId": "dhs",
  "org": "Department of Homeland Security (DHS)",
  "dep": "Office of International Affairs and Foreign Policy. Coast Guard",
  "es": "Dana W. Grady ejerce como asesora regional para América Latina (Regional Advisor for Latin America) en la Oficina de Asuntos Internacionales de la Guardia Costera de los Estados Unidos. Antes fue jefa del centro de comando del Sector St. Petersburg y coordinadora de misiones de búsqueda y rescate (Search Mission Coordinator, LCDR). Su equipo ganó el premio Geoff Pagels SAR Professionals of the Year (2025) al rescatar en el huracán Milton a Joseph Tessa, del pesquero Captain Dave, hallado en una hielera a 15 millas de la costa tras 20 horas de volcar con vientos de 75 a 90 nudos y olas de 20 a 25 pies, mediante SAROPS, deriva inversa, un MH-60, un C-144 y dos MH-65 Dolphin, tras evacuar al Sector Mobile. En el plano personal, destaca su dedicación a salvar vidas en el mar.",
  "en": "Dana W. Grady serves as Regional Advisor for Latin America in the Office of International Affairs of the United States Coast Guard (USCG). Previously, as a Lieutenant Commander (LCDR), she was chief of the Sector St. Petersburg Command Center and a Search Mission Coordinator (SMC). Her team earned the Geoff Pagels SAR Professionals of the Year award (2025) for planning and coordinating the rescue of Joseph Tessa from the fishing vessel Captain Dave during Hurricane Milton. Found alive in a fishing cooler 15 miles offshore, 20 hours after capsizing in 75-to-90-knot winds and 20-to-25-foot seas, he was rescued using SAROPS planning, reverse drift modeling, an MH-60 helicopter, a C-144 aircraft, and two MH-65 Dolphin helicopters, even after relocating to Sector Mobile. On a personal level, her career exemplifies an enduring dedication to saving lives at sea."
 }
